course: dictionary
lesson: 10976
title: Guards at the gate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A warder is a person whose job is to guard something. The clues around the word show you what kind of guard. «The castle had a hollow-sounding drawbridge, a spiky portcullis, mastiffs, and ancient warders dozing over a pasty.» A castle with a drawbridge and gates has guards, so a warder must be a guard.
explain: Clues can also show what a warder does. «On the way out the warders chaff him, teasing him with jokes.» Chaff is a hard word, but the sentence tells you that warders watch people and tease them as they leave. Then do the swap check: put guard in place of warder and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Warder has two meanings. One is a prison guard. The other is a person who guards a gate or a building. In «warders waving halberds» the warders are guards in a castle, not in a prison. The clues tell you which meaning fits.
fact: A warder is a person who guards. In old stories you will often meet warders at castles and prisons.
fact: «Warder» means a prison guard, or a person who guards a gate or a building.
fact: Today a prison guard is usually called a prison officer.
fact: At the Tower of London, the famous Beefeaters are called Yeoman Warders.
fact: A halberd is a long pole with an axe-blade on the end, and warders in old tales carried them.
exercise: «The castle had ancient warders dozing over a pasty.» What is a warder? | A kind of pasty | A bird that sings at night | A tired horse | *A person who guards a gate, a castle or a prison
exercise: Today the job title for a prison guard is what? | Bell ringer | Castle knight | Yeoman Warder | *Prison officer
exercise: At the Tower of London, the famous Beefeaters are called what? | Castle cooks | *Yeoman Warders | Prison officers | Royal horse riders
test: In The Wind in the Willows, which place do the warders guard? | A river bank | A school playground | *The castle where Toad is imprisoned | A village shop
test: Put guard in place of warder: «Men like ancient warders, waving halberds.» Does it still make sense? | Only if you add a pasty | No, it makes no sense at all | Yes, but only in a bakery | *Yes, guards waving halberds still makes sense
exam: Mia asks what a warder is. Which answer is best? | A kind of pasty | A kind of horse | *A person who guards a prison, gate or building | A king's servant who sings
